Hmm. That would probably require an overview of how the NetHack
intrinsic system is coded. Warning: C ahead. Hopefully not too
involved (from my point of view, too...)

The u structure contains all the persistent information about the
character. One of its elements is 'uprops', which stores details on
the properties the character possesses, from whatever source.

u.uprops is, in turn, an array of structures; one structure for each
property that needs to be kept track of in this manner. Some of these
are what are classically thought of as "intrinsics" (resistances, see
invisible, teleport control, etc), others are more usually referred to
as "statuses" (confusion, hallucination, blindness); there's no
difference to the game.

Each of these individual structures contains three bitfields:
u.uprops[FOO].extrinsic , .intrinsic, and .blocked. In the general case,
if any bit in either of the first two bitfields is set and none of the
bits in the third is, the character is deemed to possess the relevant
property; _which_ bit is set only matters in certain cases.

(There are standardised shortcuts for checking this; 'EFoo' is an
abbreviation for u.uprops[FOO].extrinsic, 'HFoo' for .intrinsic,
'BFoo' for .blocked. 'Foo' is the union of 'EFoo' and 'HFoo'. No, I
don't know why 'H' should denote "intrinsic" either. These shortcuts
don't exist for all properties; they're only defined when they're actually
in use. In some cases, there's no extrinsic form of the property,
and 'Foo' is used for .intrinsic (eg, Glib, Stoned, Vomiting); I call
this a wart, but I'm not on the DevTeam :-)

The extrinsic bitfield maps to the various extrinsic sources; if you
have several extrinsic sources of a property, several bits will be
set. So, for example, wearing an elven cloak will set the W_ARMC bit
of u.uprops[STEALTH].extrinsic; elven boots will set W_ARMF
(footwear); a ring of stealth will set W_RINGL or W_RINGR, depending
on which hand it's being worn (W_RING is an abbreviation for "either
of these bits"); carrying the Heart of Ahriman will set W_ART.
Exactly the same scheme is used for the blocked bitfield (though there
only happens to be two _uses_ of this in the game: mummy wrappings
blocking invisibility, and cornuthaums blocking clairvoyance for
non-Wizards).

The intrinsic bitfield is more complicated. The bottom 24 bits
(TIMEOUT) are used to store the number of turns left before a timered
property will expire (so, quaffing a non-blessed potion of invisibility
will increase the TIMEOUT value of u.uprops[INVIS].intrinsic by 31-45
turns), decremented each turn. (Note the difference between this and
timers, which store the _turn number_ on which something will occur (a
corpse rotting away, an egg hatching, etc).) The top bits are used for
"permanently" intrinsically-possessed properties, of which three types
currently exist: FROMEXPER and FROMRACE, which are set when you reach
an appropriate experience level (or from the start) as a character of a
particular role or race; and FROMOUTSIDE, which is any other source
("/* By corpses, prayer, thrones, etc. */"); the collective abbreviation
for these three is INTRINSIC. So, quaffing a blessed potion of
invisibility will set the FROMOUTSIDE bit of u.uprops[INVIS].intrinsic.

(There's a further magic bit, I_SPECIAL, used to denote that an
intrinsic property is togglable off by the player; the only time it
gets used is for blessed potions of levitation.)


So, what difference does all this make? Most actual _uses_ of a
property only care about whether at least one of the extrinsic or
intrinsic bits is set (that is, whether 'Foo' is true), though some
properties have different effects depending on whether they're
intrinsic or extrinsic (telepathy, for example). Mostly the
differences are important for handling the various means granting or
removing an intrinsic (setting the FROMOUTSIDE bit twice doesn't
matter, whereas setting both W_ARMC and W_ARMF is redundancy; gremlins
will unset all three INTRINSIC bits, whereas level drain only unsets
FROMEXPER and FROMRACE).


I've glossed over some of the finer details (such as how this
interacts with properties of your monster form when polyselfed,
fumbling (which is an extrinsic source but has a timeout associated
with it), wounded legs (which notes _which_ leg as a ring source),
...), but this may be helpful. Let me know if it isn't, or if I've
made any silly mistakes :-)

